# Two lotteries {#sec-intro}

Every year Nevada and New Mexico have more people who want to hunt mule deer than tags to give, so each holds a lottery. Nevada remembers: an unsuccessful applicant earns a bonus point, her points are squared to fix how many random numbers are drawn for her the next year, and her lowest number is her entry, so ten years of losing gives her a hundred and one draws against a newcomer's one.^[Nevada Department of Wildlife, "Bonus Point Program," https://www.ndow.org/blog/bonus-point-program/.] New Mexico forgets: "New Mexico does NOT grant preference to applicants who were unsuccessful in previous drawings."^[New Mexico Department of Game and Fish, "How the New Mexico Draw Works," https://wildlife.dgf.nm.gov/hunting/applications-and-draw-information/how-new-mexico-draw-works/.] A hunter passed over for ten years enters the eleventh draw as a first-time entrant.

Many institutions side with Nevada, though they disagree about how: Arizona adds one random number per unsuccessful year; Colorado gives most licences to whoever has the most preference points, chance only breaking ties. Others forget, among them the lotteries that allocate some 85,000 H-1B visas and up to 55,000 Diversity Visas a year: a worker not selected in three successive years enters the fourth draw as a newcomer, even under the rule in force since February 2026, which weights registrations by the wage offered.^[Department of Homeland Security, "Weighted Selection Process for Registrants and Petitioners Seeking To File Cap-Subject H-1B Petitions," 90 *Federal Register* 60864 (2025).]

The theory of fair allocation has barely touched this disagreement, and where it does, sides with New Mexico. The best-developed account, John Broome's [-@Broome1984; -@Broome1991a; -@Broome1991b], holds that fairness requires claims to be satisfied in proportion to their strength, and that when the good is indivisible and claims are equal, an equal lottery is the fairest available procedure. It evaluates a lottery on its own, and on its own each year's New Mexico draw is impeccable. Broome's critics, who locate the fairness of lotteries in what they express or exclude rather than in the chances they distribute [@Wasserman1996; @Stone2007; @Stone2011; @Spiekermann2022], reach the same verdict: an impartial procedure is fair whatever last year's did. The wider literature takes the single lottery as its unit [@Sher1980; @KornhauserSager1988; @Elster1988; @Duxbury1999; @Saunders2008; @Stone2008]. The nearest to my thesis is a page of Hersch and Rowe's [-@HerschRowe2025, 3]: "many goods can be divided temporally as well as spatially," so that an indivisible good can be divided "over time through a rotation," which they credit to Elster [-@Elster1989], and for recurring chores "who completed the chore last time matters to who has a claim to avoid it this time." That is the divisibility argument in miniature. In computational social choice [@Lackner2020; @LacknerMaly2023; @FreemanZahediConitzer2017; @Benade2018] history-dependence is an axiom. What follows adds the derivation inside Broome's theory, against his own extinction thesis; the constraint on how hard memory may press; and the structure in which it is forbidden.

I will argue for three claims. *Non-separability*: the fairness of a sequence of allocations is not a function of its members' fairness as the standard theory assesses it. *Memory*: when units of a good are allocated repeatedly among claimants who remain in the pool after receiving one, fairness requires that each claimant's chances in later rounds depend on her history of unsatisfied claims. *Currency and weight*: what memory tracks is a claimant's shortfall from her fair share, in units of the good where a later unit is as good to her as an earlier one and in waiting where it is not; and it may press that shortfall only as hard as leaves no claimant's expected share, over whatever stay she turns out to have, below what her claim warrants.

# Claims, chances, and division {#sec-broome}

A claim, in Broome's sense, is a duty owed to the candidate herself that she should have the good, as distinct from other reasons for giving it to her, such as usefulness [@Broome1991a, 91–93]. Fairness requires that "equal claims require equal satisfaction," that "stronger claims require more satisfaction than weaker ones," and that "weaker claims must not simply be overridden by stronger ones" [@Broome1991a, 95].

Two features of the theory carry my argument. The first I will call the *Division Principle*: where a good is divisible, proportional satisfaction is achievable and required: if two people have equal claims to some good, it "should be divided," even when giving it all to one of them would do more good [@Broome1991a, 95]. Broome states it most generally in a passage that already divides across time:

> The good, for instance, of avoiding military service could be divided by conscripting everyone for a short time rather than a few people for a long time. If people's claims to a good are equal, then fairness requires that the good should be divided between them if it can be. That is the only way to treat them with perfect equality. [@Broome1984, 47]

The second is the account of lotteries as second best. When the good is indivisible and scarce, a lottery gives "a sort of partial equality in satisfaction": "Each person can be given a sort of surrogate satisfaction. By holding a lottery, each can be given an equal chance of getting the good. This is not a perfect fairness, but it meets the requirement of fairness to some extent" [@Broome1991a, 97–98]. Broome grants this only if a chance counts as surrogate satisfaction, which he finds plausible (98). Two things follow. Where division is available, it is required. Where it is not, the surrogate is owed: a claimant who cannot be given her portion must be given her chance.

What duty does Nevada owe anyone to let her shoot a deer? Broome declines to say which reasons are claims, beyond granting that some are [@Broome1991a, 93]. The claims in my cases arise from the allocator's undertaking: a body that announces it will allocate a public resource fairly among eligible applicants thereby owes each of them fair treatment, and what that requires is not the agency's to stipulate. New Mexico's announcement that it grants no preference to past losers describes its procedure, not its fairness.

# The divisibility argument {#sec-division}

Consider a good that comes in indivisible units, one per period, allocated by one agency among equal claims, where receiving a unit does not remove a claimant from the pool: a tag a year, a night off the rota a week. Suppose for now that the same $N$ claimants are present for $T$ periods. Within any one period the standard verdict is correct: one unit, $N$ equal claims, no division, so an equal lottery. But the units are indivisible and the sequence is not. Over $T$ periods it is possible to give each claimant $T/N$ units, or as near as the integers allow. Call the procedure that does so *rotation*: draw a random order once and cycle through it. Rotation is, in the sequence, what dividing the food is in the single case. The sequence can be divided, so fairness requires rotation, or whatever comes as close to it as circumstances allow.

Compare New Mexico's procedure, a fresh equal lottery every period: the *forgetting* rule. Over $T$ periods it does not divide the sequence but raffles it. With ten claimants and ten periods it leaves, on average, three and a half of them with nothing while others receive two or more units.^[The expected number with nothing is $N(1-1/N)^T$. The simulation code behind every figure in this paper accompanies it.] Rotation leaves nobody with nothing. The forgetting rule does over the sequence what the theory forbids in the single case: it takes a divisible good and gives it out by lot.

As a reductio: suppose someone proposed to allocate a cake among ten people with equal claims by cutting it into ten slices and raffling each slice separately. Each raffle, taken by itself, is what the theory recommends for an indivisible good. Nobody thinks this a fair way to allocate a cake; the theory itself says the cake should be divided, which means giving everyone a slice, not a chance at each. A cake handed out over ten days is still a divisible cake.

Two consequences follow. The first is non-separability. The standard theory assesses a lottery by the claims present when it is held, fixed by need, desert and the like, not by earlier lotteries. Call a rule *locally fair* at a period if its procedure then, so assessed, is fair. The forgetting rule is locally fair at every period; rotation at none after the first, since given the history one claimant is certain of the unit. Yet rotation is the fair allocation of the sequence and the forgetting rule is not. So fairness, so assessed, is not separable across occasions. Put another way, as I will from @sec-extinction on: if claims carry history, so that a claimant who has received less than her share has a stronger claim to the next unit, then rotation *is* locally fair at every period and fairness is separable after all. These are one thesis in two vocabularies; what is not available is history-blind claims *and* separable fairness.

The second consequence is memory. Any rule that divides the sequence must give units to those who have received fewer, which requires knowing who they are.

The argument assumes something an opponent will press. The Division Principle applies to a good that "could be divided," and the tags a hunter draws in different years are not portions of one tag. What licenses treating them so is *Fungibility*: units allocated in different periods are portions of one divisible good, for a claimant, to the extent that her claim is to a unit of that kind and a later unit would satisfy it as an earlier one would. The hunter's claim is to a tag, not to the 2027 tag. Fungibility is a matter of degree, since delay is nearly always some cost, and it fails outright when a later unit is much worse for the claimant: the visa applicant whose sponsor will not wait. Then the claim is largely to timeliness. But timeliness is divisible: delay can be shared out as units cannot, so the argument changes currency. Fairness requires that the *waiting* be divided in proportion to claims, where the constraint of @sec-proportion allows it (@sec-bottleneck).^[With unequal claims the problem is apportionment: claims problems are apportionment problems [@WinteinHeilmann2018], and divisor methods have a sequential form that awards seats one at a time by a rank index depending on the tally so far [@BalinskiYoung2001, 100, Prop. 3.3]. "Repeated" fair division [@Igarashi2024; cf. @AleksandrovWalsh2020] concerns envy over many rounds, not claims.]

# The extinction objection {#sec-extinction}

The argument seems to run into a doctrine Broome states in the same year. Once a fair lottery has given Michael the dialysis machine, it would be absurd to hold another on the ground that lotteries are fair: "once Michael has won the first lottery he has a perfect claim to the treatment, and Maggie has none. Consequently it is not at all fair to hold a second lottery" [@Broome1984b, 628–629]. Call this the *extinction thesis*: losing a fair lottery extinguishes the loser's claim. Vong [-@Vong2015] keeps the thesis in one form: a *procedural* claim, once satisfied by a fair lottery, is satisfied, while a *benefit* claim "cannot be lost due to the results of a procedurally fair lottery" (pp. 479–480); so when new units arrive the losers are "a new case of scarce benefit distribution" and get a fresh fair lottery (p. 483). On either form the persistent loser is owed nothing but a fresh lottery among all who remain, which is the forgetting rule; and where the literature does give a lottery memory [@FeldblyumLeBlevennec2023], it is the *winner's* standing that is protected when new claimants arrive.

The reply distinguishes three structures the objection runs together. A *re-run*: the same claimants, the same unit, a second lottery because the first went the wrong way for someone. Broome is right that this is absurd, because there is nothing left to divide. An *interrupted allocation*, Vong's case: one unit, and new claimants arriving before it is handed over. And *recurrence*: a new unit each period, among overlapping claimants. Here the extinction thesis is true and irrelevant. Maggie's claim to the 2026 tag was extinguished when she lost; it is someone else's. But there is a 2027 tag, and the Division Principle says the two together are a divisible good, so the 2027 tag goes, or is more likely to go, to someone who did not get the 2026 one. It is a claim to a unit that exists, with more force than a past winner's for the same reason that, when a cake is divided, someone who has had no slice has more of her claim outstanding than someone who has had one. Where the currency is waiting and everyone will be served, the same holds with waiting in place of slices.

To bite, Broome's objection needs a stronger thesis: that losing a fair lottery for one unit extinguishes any bearing the loss might have on later units. That is what the cake refutes. Someone who raffled each slice could say of each raffle that the losers' claims to that slice were fairly extinguished, and the cake would still not have been divided. Vong's form fails for a related reason: a procedural claim is satisfied by a fresh equal lottery only if a fresh equal lottery is the fair procedure, and over recurring units the Division Principle says it is not. Vong rejects views on which losing weakens a claim and on which winning strengthens it (pp. 477–479); I find no discussion in his paper of the option that losing leaves more of the claim outstanding for *later* units, which his objections, about new claimants for a single unit, do not touch.

A residual worry makes the surrogate character of chances do work. If the 2026 lottery gave Maggie surrogate satisfaction, does extra weight in 2027 not satisfy her twice? A chance is not a portion of the good but what one gives when a portion cannot be given. If an equal chance of a slice were a kind of equal slice, the raffle of the cake would be a division of the cake, and the theory could not say that the cake should be divided instead. What memory counts is units received, not chances. Wasserman's complaint that claimants cannot eat chances [-@Wasserman1996, 47, 49], pressed against calling a chance a partial satisfaction, is on this view the beginning of an argument for memory, not an objection to lotteries.

# What memory tracks {#sec-proportion}

Sort the institutions by structure. In *recurrence* a claimant who receives a unit stays in the pool: tags, permits, chores. In a *bottleneck* she leaves: a visa, a flat, a kidney. (Hersch and Rowe use "bottleneck" more narrowly, for queues everyone eventually clears.) Structure is fixed by whether winners leave and currency by Fungibility, and in practice they run together, since whoever leaves on being served was waiting for one unit and cared when it came.

## Shortfall in units

In recurrence the argument of @sec-division fixes what memory tracks: the difference between the share of units a claimant should have received by now and the number she has, her *shortfall*. With equal claims and one unit per period, a claimant present for $r$ periods who has received $w$ units has shortfall $r/N - w$. Shortfall is neither the number of losses nor time waited: points-for-losses systems reset on a win, treating a claimant who won once in twenty years as a newcomer, and count a loss among two hundred applicants as one among two; time waited tracks shortfall only if units and claimants are constant in number.

Consider the rules that press shortfall with a weight $\kappa \geq 0$: each claimant's chance is $1/N$ plus $\kappa/N$ times the excess of her standing over the pool's mean, floored at zero, where standing is shortfall kept relative to the pool: a newcomer enters level with the pool's mean, and a claimant who leaves takes her standing with her (@sec-reach). At $\kappa = 0$ this is the forgetting rule. At $\kappa = 1$ a claimant's distance from the mean shrinks by about a factor of $1 - 1/N$ each period, so it is corrected over roughly the time the practice takes to hand out one round of shares. A past winner's chance falls below a newcomer's, and reaches zero once she is $1/\kappa$ units ahead of the pool; she is then excluded until the others catch up, but never taxed, which is the extinction thesis built in. As $\kappa$ grows the rule approaches giving each unit to whoever is furthest behind, which, when all arrived together, is Aleksandrov et al.'s [-@Aleksandrov2015] Balanced Like, a lottery among those who have received fewest. In a fixed population everyone starts level, so each has the same chance of every unit whatever $\kappa$ is, while dispersion falls: its variance is $\frac{N-1}{2\kappa N}$ absent clipping, and with ten claimants and ten periods the expected number left with nothing falls from 3.5 under the forgetting rule to 2.1 at $\kappa = 1$, 1.4 at $\kappa = 2$ and 0 at $\kappa = 10$. In a fixed population the Division Principle says: press as hard as you can, and rotate.

The rule has near relatives. Lackner's [-@Lackner2020] perpetual voting rules weight voters by the history of past decisions, since "only due to this dependency we can achieve fairness over time" (2104); his Perpetual Quota weights each by $\max(0,\, \text{quota} - \text{satisfaction})$ (2106), which in my setting is shortfall. But his rules are deterministic, so here Perpetual Quota *is* rotation in its tie-breaking order, and the memoryless lottery enters only as a benchmark (2109). The lottery with memory lies between, and the theory of claims requires it, since an arbitrary fixed order among equal claims is what it forbids. Rules weighting by losses alone fail even simple proportionality [@Lackner2020, Prop. 1; @LacknerMaly2023, Thm. 2], a deterministic analogue of the failure of points-for-losses. And when every unit is worth the same to everyone, the forgetting rule's envy between two claimants is a random walk growing as $\sqrt{T/N}$ while rotation holds it below one unit; that no rule beats random allocation in the worst case [@Benade2018, Thms. 2.1, 2.13] needs an adversary who values a unit differently for different claimants, which is where Fungibility fails. How heavily memory may press when claimants come and go, Lackner leaves open (2109).

## Newcomers and the surrogate constraint

Once claimants come and go, a real objection appears. A newcomer has as much of her claim outstanding as any past loser had on her own first application, and may be gone before the division reaches her; under any rule with memory she is given a smaller chance than that loser now has.

The answer also fixes how heavily memory may press. Division of the sequence is available only to a claimant who stays long enough for it to be completed; one who leaves after three periods cannot be given three-tenths of a tag, and for her the sequence is as indivisible as the unit. If the allocator knew who would leave, it would owe such claimants the surrogate outright: a chance, each period, proportional to their claim. Not knowing, it owes the surrogate to everyone, in the only form available: that no claimant's expected share, over whatever tenure she turns out to have, fall short of proportionality. Call this the *surrogate constraint*. It is measured over the chances the rule assigns, exit taken as given; a claimant who leaves because she lost has a complaint against her luck, not the rule. And it is not a consideration to be weighed against the dispersion that memory reduces, because the two stand differently to the claimants. The chance is what a claimant who will not stay for the division is *owed*: a rule that gives her less partly overrides her claim. Dispersion among those who stay is the residue of the lottery that indivisibility made necessary, and a lottery's residue is what the standard theory already *tolerates*; reducing it is an improvement in degree. So a rule may not be designed to buy an improvement of the second kind with a harm of the first: it may not transfer expected share away from any tenure class, as points and queues do. That ranking is mine, not Broome's, though it rests on his distinction between a claim overridden and a claim met in part; and since the floor is owed to each claimant who may leave, not to a class, their number does not bear on whether it is owed. Within it, those who stay are owed as much division as can be had.

Three things hold of this constraint when exit is independent of history. First, it can only be met exactly: chances sum to one in every period, so under any rule the pool's expected share is proportional, and no tenure class can stand above proportionality unless another stands below it. Memory may redistribute realized shortfall across tenures, never expected share. Second, the shortfall rule meets it, for every $\kappa$, so long as no chance is clipped at zero. A newcomer enters level with the pool, so her chance in her first period is $1/N$ whatever the pool's state; thereafter her expected distance from the pool mean shrinks each period and acquires no constant term, since exits independent of history leave the pool's expected standing where it was. So every tenure class receives exactly its share. Third, what memory can do has a closed form. With exit rate $q$ the variance of shortfall at exit is about $\frac{N-1}{N(Nq + 2\kappa(1-q))}$, so the reduction relative to forgetting depends on $\kappa$ and on the number of units a claimant can expect to receive during a stay, $1/Nq$. Where a claimant can expect one unit during her stay, as in @tbl-churn, $\kappa = 1$ removes two-fifths of the dispersion; where a hundred claimants compete for each unit and stay ten years, under a tenth; as units per stay vanish, the verdict of this section shades into that of @sec-bottleneck.

What limits $\kappa$ is clipping, where the residue the constraint tolerates reappears. While $\kappa \leq N/(N-1)$ no single win clips a chance; runs of wins do, and a clipped winner's unpaid share then falls on everyone, the newcomer included. That transfer is not designed but forced by the unit's indivisibility, of the kind the standard theory tolerates wherever it tolerates a lottery; the constraint forbids the first and sets $\kappa$ where the second remains the residue of luck rather than of the rule's normal operation (@tbl-churn).

