Clipping costs the newcomer nothing at $\kappa = 1/2$, under one per cent at $\kappa = 1$, six per cent at $\kappa = 2$ and nearly half her chance at $\kappa = 10$, against reductions in dispersion of a quarter, two-fifths, a half and over a half; beyond $N/(N-1)$ the rule clips on a first win, and the transfer is its design.

The rules in use breach the constraint outright. Linear points give a newcomer a quarter of the chance her claim warrants and a claimant who stays thirty periods 15 per cent more than her share; squared points cut the newcomer to a twelfth; a queue gives her nothing, and whoever leaves early leaves with nothing. These rules reduce dispersion, some by more than the moderate shortfall rules, but by transferring expected share from short tenures to long, which denies the short-stayers the surrogate they are owed.

So, in recurrence, the third claim is established. Memory is required: up to $N/(N-1)$ every tenure class keeps its share, up to the residue of luck, and dispersion falls, so the forgetting rule is dominated. But to remember lexically lets an incumbent's accumulated claim override a newcomer's present one. When the pool turns over, fairness requires a rule that raises chances with shortfall at a rate that corrects it over roughly the period in which the practice delivers a share, and no faster than $N/(N-1)$; the rules in use lie far outside that region. Such a rule removes some two-fifths of the dispersion the forgetting rule produces; the residual lottery is the surrogate the short-stayers are owed. Only where the pool is fixed is it zero, and there the rule is rotation.

## Bottlenecks and waiting {#sec-bottleneck}

In a bottleneck nobody accumulates more than one unit; what varies is time waited. Fungibility fails, because each claim is to one unit and to having it soon; what is divisible is the waiting. Under the forgetting rule, waiting is raffled: with a pool of ten kept full by arrivals and one unit per period, about one in seventy waits forty periods or more, though everyone's expected wait is nine. A queue serves everyone at nine. Time, unlike a flat, is divisible, so the Division Principle applies to the waiting and says: equalize it. This is why John and Millum [-@JohnMillum2020, 181, 205–206] are right that a queue can be as fair as a lottery, and Hersch and Rowe [-@HerschRowe2024] right to separate "bottleneck" cases, where queues belong, from "scarcity" cases, where lotteries do; between them lies recurrence, where a queue gives a short-stayer nothing.

The surrogate constraint carries over, and here it cuts the other way. Where claimants may leave unserved, by dying or losing eligibility, and the allocator cannot tell who, those to whom the division is not available are owed the surrogate: an equal chance, each period, at the unit. A pure queue denies it outright; so, to a degree, does any rule that weights by time waited. In recurrence the newcomer's floor could be met because past winners hold a surplus and can be pushed below her; in a bottleneck nobody holds a surplus, since winners leave, so every unit of chance given to a long waiter is taken, by design, from a claim still wholly outstanding. With exits unforeseeable and uncorrelated with waiting, the only rule under which no tenure class's expected treatment falls below the equal chance is the forgetting rule. So the result is two-sided a second way. Where everyone will be served, the waiting is divisible and fairness requires a queue; where some will leave unserved and the allocator cannot tell who, fairness requires the lottery that forgets. Memory is required in one structure and forbidden in the other; the difference is whether there are surplus-holders to pay for it. Housing lists and transplant lists, where some always leave unserved, fall on the second side so far as exit is unforeseeable. Where it is foreseeable, or rises with waiting, the claim is urgent, time waited is evidence of urgency, and the rule must weight it, as transplant allocation does: a difference in claims, not a memory of history. (Bighorn ram tags, which New Mexico issues once in a lifetime,^[19.31.17.9 NMAC (2025).] are a bottleneck; there New Mexico's rule is right.)

The verdict for the visa lotteries follows, and it is not what the opening suggests. They are bottlenecks with attrition: a selected worker leaves the pool, and an unselected one may lose her sponsor or status unforeseeably. So the lottery that forgets is the fair rule, and Hersch and Rowe's [-@HerschRowe2024, 188] endorsement of the Diversity Visa lottery is right, though not because the case is scarcity rather than bottleneck: the surrogate owed to those who will leave unserved cannot be paid by anyone else. What is wrong with the rule in force since 2026 is not that it forgets but that it weights by wage, a reason of usefulness rather than a claim. And their remark that a periodic lottery in a bottleneck is unfair because a person who "arrives into the lottery pool early and is continuously unlucky" may wait far longer than a lucky newcomer (199) holds only where everyone will be served.

# How far does memory reach? {#sec-reach}

If Nevada must remember Maggie's 2026 loss in 2027, must it remember her loss in the housing lottery? In Arizona's draw? A view that cannot stop somewhere principled collapses into a demand that luck be equalized, a different and more contested thesis. Two bounds, besides the bound in weight from @sec-proportion, follow from the argument that generates the claim.

*Memory is bound to the practice.* The corrective claim arises because a particular agency's allocation of a particular good left residual unfairness: a claim on *those* units, addressed to *that* agency. The good matters because the 2027 tag can divide the sequence only because it is a tag; a flat is no portion of what the hunter has a claim to. Goods are individuated by claims, not names: a Nevada tag and an Arizona tag are different goods, since a claim to hunt in one state is not satisfied by a tag for the other, but two draws run by one agency for the same tag are one good, so an allocator cannot shed its obligation by splitting a practice. The agency matters because dividing is something an agent does with what it has; another allocator has none of its units. This distinguishes the thesis from luck egalitarianism [@Dworkin1981; @Cohen1989; @Temkin1993], whose currency is compensation, so that a shortfall in one good can be made up in another; mine concerns one agency's division of one good, in its currency, and is silent on whether the loser is worse off overall.

*Memory is bound to the claim.* The corrective claim is a strengthened claim to the good, not a credit. Someone who applied unsuccessfully for ten years and then gave up hunting is owed nothing; there is no tag she has a claim to. Memory is not compensation: shortfall arises under the fairest rule, since the residual lottery produces it, so correcting it presupposes no wrong. A boundary question follows. *Transition*: when a forgetting practice adopts memory, the whole record counts, wins and losses, since the shortfall existed whether or not the rule tracked it; the switch after a long forgetting history is steep in proportion to the unfairness it ends. A rectificatory view would count only the losses.

# Objections {#sec-objections}

*Losing on purpose.* That memory rewards losing is a familiar objection: the NBA moved from a worst-first draft order to a lottery because of the incentive to lose [@Elster1988, 123 n. 46]. In my cases losing is not under the claimant's control: she can only apply, and applying without a genuine claim is the problem of verifying claims, which every allocation faces. Where losing is controllable, as in a league, fairness is in tension with incentive, a reason to weigh fairness against other things, not evidence that fairness requires forgetting. What must be conceded is that memory raises the value of a manufactured claim: a speculative entrant gains a ticket that grows. The response is to attach memory to participation that costs something, as a paid licence to apply does; a constraint on implementing fairness, not evidence that fairness has no memory.

*Equality, not fairness.* Someone might grant the verdict and deny that fairness is what condemns the outcomes: what is bad is inequality, or that some are badly off [@Parfit2000]. Suppose the persistent loser is, all things considered, no worse off than the repeat winner. Her complaint survives: this agency, dividing this good among equal claims, has given her less than her share. That is comparative, concerns the good in question, and is addressed to the allocator, the marks of a fairness complaint in Broome's sense; a priority view cannot explain why it lapses when she stops claiming the good.

*Ex ante.* The last objection comes from the view, descending from Diamond's [-@Diamond1967] reply to Harsanyi [-@Harsanyi1955] [@Frick2015; @OtsukaVoorhoeve2009], that a procedure's fairness is a matter of the prospects it gives people at the time of choice. On that view the forgetting rule and rotation are equally fair, since each claimant starts with the same prospects under both; the forgetting rule's defect, as the first row of @tbl-churn showed, is entirely ex post. First reply: the ex ante view must say when the ex ante is. If it is the beginning of the practice, the view cannot prefer the forgetting rule to rotation, which are ex ante identical, and the Division Principle settles what it leaves open. If it is the beginning of each period, the view asserts separability with respect to history-blind claims, which the cake refutes. Second, my view is ex ante exactly where the ex ante view is right: a claimant is owed chances insofar as division is unavailable to her and units insofar as it is, which is why the surrogate constraint is indexed to her entry. The ex ante view was built for one-shot cases, where division never is.

# Conclusion {#sec-conclusion}

Fairness has memory where the good recurs. What the memory tracks is a claimant's shortfall from her share of the good being divided; it is owed by the agency dividing the good, for as long as she has a claim to it; and it may be pressed only as hard as leaves no claimant's expected share, over whatever stay she has, below what her claim warrants. Where winners leave and some will leave unserved, the same constraint forbids memory, because no one holds a surplus to pay for it. The result was visible in Broome's own illustration of the Division Principle: military service, divided by conscripting everyone for a short time. Everyone's short time is a rotation. The lottery is what one does when one cannot rotate, and one can rotate more often than its defenders have noticed.

